Cake custom kernel: all-in-one experimental patch for CachyOS 7.2.9
=====================================================================

Base:   CachyOS release tarball cachyos-7.2.9-1 (Makefile 7.2.9).
Apply:  one Kernel Manager patch entry, or from the source root:
          patch -Np1 --fuzz=0 < this-file
Status: experimental. Applies to cachyos-7.2.9-1 without offsets or fuzz.
        Every object it touches compiles with the running 7.2.9-1-cachyos-custom
        config (clang 23.1.1, LLVM=1, ThinLTO) with no warnings. Not linked,
        booted or run as a whole yet.

Sections 1-3 are 0001-cake-kernel-full-signals-experimental-cachyos-7.2.8.patch
(the kernel installed on 2026-10-04), split by feature without code changes.
Section 4 is new (2026-10-05). Each section is a separate diff against the
tree the sections before it produce, so a file can appear in more than one
section. Apply them in order.

Index
-----

Section 1  BPF: native scalar bit operations for BPF programs
  include/linux/filter.h       declarations of bpf_ctz64/bpf_popcnt64/
                               bpf_clz64/bpf_mulhi64
  kernel/bpf/helpers.c         the four kfuncs (exact C fallbacks) and their
                               BTF IDs; CTZ/POPCNT/CLZ are KF_FASTCALL on
                               x86-64 with the JIT
  kernel/bpf/verifier.c        special-kfunc IDs, fastcall for the unary
                               ops, and return bounds (0..64) so callers can
                               index without masking
  arch/x86/net/bpf_jit_comp.c  emit_scalar_bitop(): TZCNT/POPCNT/LZCNT and
                               MUL emitted inline instead of a call when the
                               CPU has BMI1/POPCNT/ABM
  tools/testing/selftests/bpf  scalar_bitops selftest (new files)
  Switch: none. Cake detects the kfuncs at start (native CTZ/POPCNT is its
  default; --portable-bitops compares against the builtins).

Section 2  sched_ext: DSQ lookup
  kernel/sched/ext/ext.c       dsq_cmpfn(): typed u64 compare for the DSQ
                               hash; a direct index for DSQ IDs below 2048
                               (find_user_dsq()), kept in step with the hash
                               under dsq_index_lock in scx_bpf_create_dsq()
                               and destroy_dsq(); freed with the scheduler;
                               sysfs dsq_direct_entries
  kernel/sched/ext/internal.h  struct scx_sched: dsq_index_lock, dsq_direct
  Switch: kernel command line sched_ext.dsq_direct=0 keeps hash-only lookup.
  Cake uses it automatically (all its DSQ IDs are below 2048).

Section 3  Wake signals: input and GPU-fence causes on wakeups, IRQ time
  include/linux/sched/wake_signal.h  wake-flag bits for the two causes (new)
  kernel/sched/sched.h, core.c       record the cause on an accepted blocked
                                     wake (try_to_wake_up(), wake_up_process())
  kernel/sched/wait.c                carry it through __wake_up() and
                                     __wake_up_locked_key()
  fs/eventpoll.c, fs/eventfd.c,      forward it through epoll and eventfd
  include/linux/eventfd.h
  drivers/input/evdev.c              tag input wakes at SYN_REPORT
  drivers/dma-buf/dma-fence.c,       tag fence-completion wakes
  drivers/dma-buf/sync_file.c,
  drivers/gpu/drm/drm_syncobj.c
  include/linux/sched/ext.h          per-task token p->scx.wake_signal
  kernel/sched/ext/ext.c, ext.h,     SCX_OPS_WAKE_SIGNAL (bit 9) opt-in, token
  internal.h                         cleared at running, kfuncs
                                     scx_bpf_task_wake_signal_v1() and
                                     scx_bpf_cpu_irq_ns_v1()
  Switch: scheduler opt-in. Cake: --kernel-signals observe|on.

Section 4  sched_ext: lean idle claim (K1-K4)
  kernel/sched/ext/internal.h  SCX_OPS_LEAN_IDLE (bit 10) and
                               SCX_OPS_LEAN_IDLE_LAZY_SMT (bit 11), both in
                               SCX_OPS_ALL_FLAGS
  kernel/sched/ext/idle.c
    K2  struct scx_idle_line: the idle CPU word and the idle SMT word in one
        128-byte-aligned block that holds nothing else. scx_idle_enable()
        points the global masks at it for a lean scheduler, and back at the
        allocated masks otherwise, before ops.init() runs.
    K1  scx_idle_lean_claim(): a single-word claim with a precomputed
        sibling word (scx_idle_lean_sib[]). scx_bpf_test_and_clear_cpu_idle()
        takes it before RCU, the scheduler lookup and the CPU check; the
        pick paths take it through scx_idle_test_and_clear_cpu().
    K4  scx_idle_lean_update(), called from update_builtin_idle(): tests
        before every write, so the busy transition after a claim only reads.
    K3  with SCX_OPS_LEAN_IDLE_LAZY_SMT, a successful claim leaves the SMT
        word to the claimed CPU's busy transition; a failed claim still
        clears it, which keeps scx_pick_idle_cpu() from looping.
  Applies only with the global idle mask and nr_cpu_ids <= 64; otherwise the
  flags are ignored. scx_idle_disable() turns lean mode off first.
  Switch: scheduler opt-in, so a lean and a regular Cake can be compared on
  one boot. Cake: --lean-idle on|lazy.

